text:
Robots need to handle the hidden rules of the real world—think friction, mass, or even secret buttons—without being told what matters. Enter T2-Z: a method that attaches a small, trainable latent vector to a world model, letting robots adapt on the fly to new environments with just 1–2 interactions.

No hand-crafted factor lists, no ground-truth labels. Just a few seconds of adaptation and T2-Z cuts prediction errors by 2–3x and nearly triples action success rates (from ≈27%→71% real-world, 34%→74% sim). Latents naturally organize along true physical properties—even across wildly different visual scenes.

The kicker: adaptation is fast (18 seconds on a single GPU) and works with only a handful of test-time steps. T2-Z bridges lab-trained models with the unpredictable mess of reality, making robust, adaptive robotics practical.

Get the full analysis here: https://t.co/2Mzqb6Fptf

